gaKU(){return"Excellent"},
gaKV(){return"Strong risk-adjusted return \u2014 you're well compensated for the volatility you take."},
gaKW(){return"Based on your actual portfolio history \u2014 not a theoretical model. Past volatility does not predict future swings. Sharpe assumes normally distributed returns; real markets have fat tails."},
gaKX(){return"Decent risk/reward \u2014 broadly in line with a well-diversified equity portfolio."},
aKY(a){return"Volatility: measures how much your portfolio value swings day to day, expressed as an annualised percentage.\n\nSharpe Ratio: how much return you're earning per unit of risk taken, after subtracting a risk-free baseline. Higher is better. A ratio of 1.0 means you earn 1% of excess return per 1% of annual volatility.\n\nBoth use your actual recorded portfolio values \u2014 no theoretical assumptions about holdings."+a},
gaKZ(){return"Volatility is near-zero with this few data points, making the ratio statistically unreliable. It will appear as history accumulates."},
gaL_(){return"Need 5+ observations"},
